## ABSTRACT
Lithium-sulfur (Li-S) battery has attracted intensive attention in the realm of energy storage owing to its high theoretical capacity and energy density. However, the shuttle effect of soluble lithium polysulfides (LiPSs) between electrodes results in rapid capacity degradation. Herein, a strategy which combines the design of both chemical interaction and microstructure of interlayer was proposed to suppress the shuttle effect. The chemical interaction between different functionalized MOFs and LiPSs was systematically studied to find the best candidate. Furthermore, an interlayer with ordered structure was constructed via the layer-by-layer assembly of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) on graphene (UNO66-NH\(_{2}\)@graphene) to create sinuous channels which can better impede the diffusion process of LiPSs by the strong adsorption of MOF toward LiPSs. Consequently, in comparison to the battery with a bare separator, the ordered interlayer increased the initial discharge capacity of battery by 28.98% at 1.0 C and lowered the capacity decay rate remarkably from 0.10% to 0.067% per cycle, indicating that the design of chemical interaction and microstructure paves the way for high-performance Li-S batteries.
## KEYWORDS
ordered structure, interlayer, metal-organic frameworks, lithium-sulfur battery
## 1 Introduction
Lithium-sulfur (Li-S) battery has become one of the most promising rechargeable batteries owing to its high theoretical capacity (1,672 mAh·g\(^{-1}\)), high energy density (2,600 Wh·kg\(^{-1}\)), low cost and environmental friendliness [1–3]. However, its practical application has been limited by self-discharging, low Coulombic efficiency, poor cycling stability and loss of active sulfur material, which are mainly caused by the shuttle effect of intermediate soluble LiPSs (Li\(_{2}\)S\(_{x}\), 4 ≤ x ≤ 8). Specifically, during the charge/discharge process of Li-S battery, the soluble LiPSs may penetrate through the separator, diffuse to the Li metal anode, react with Li metal and eventually form insoluble Li\(_{2}\)S and Li\(_{2}\)S\(_{2}\) [4]. To address the shuttle effect, researchers have proposed solutions based on the design of sulfur host, interlayer, separator, binder and electrolyte [5–9].The design strategies of addressing shuttle effects can be mainly divided into three categories, i.e., physical barrier, chemical adsorption and catalytic conversion [10]. Physical barrier can block the LiPSs via small pore size or charge repulsion [11, 12]. Chemical adsorption can trap LiPSs via specific chemical interaction and thus suppress shuttle effects. For instance, porous carbon materials can serve as S host in cathode [13–15], and doped carbon materials can serve as interlayer to capture LiPSs [16]. Functionalized BN Nanosheets, polar materials such as TiO\(_{2}\) can interact with LiPSs and hinder their diffusion [17, 18]. Catalytic conversion mainly relies on different catalysts such as dithiothreitol, hemin molecules, iron phthalocyanine and octafluoronaphthalene, which can promote the conversion of LiPSs [19–21]. Our former researches mainly concentrate on suppressing shuttle effects via adsorption and catalytic conversion toward LiPSs, and it was found that adsorption is important for both of these two strategies [13–21]. However, traditional porous material