The design strategies of addressing shuttle effects can be mainly divided into three categories, i.e., physical barrier, chemical adsorption and catalytic conversion [10]. Physical barrier can block the LiPSs via small pore size or charge repulsion [11, 12]. Chemical adsorption can trap LiPSs via specific chemical interaction and thus suppress shuttle effects. For instance, porous carbon materials can serve as S host in cathode [13–15], and doped carbon materials can serve as interlayer to capture LiPSs [16]. Functionalized BN Nanosheets, polar materials such as TiO2 can interact with LiPSs and
hinder their diffusion [17, 18]. Catalytic conversion mainly relies on different catalysts such as dithiothreitol, hemin molecules, iron phthalocyanine and octafluoronaphthalene, which can promote the conversion of LiPSs [19–21]. Our former researches mainly concentrate on suppressing shuttle effects via adsorption and catalytic conversion toward LiPSs, and it was found that adsorption is important for both of these two strategies [13–21]. However, traditional porous materials such as carbon materials and metal oxide, have weak interaction toward LiPSs, which cannot obstruct the diffusion of LiPSs efficiently and finally lead to decay of battery performance, especially for battery with high sulfur loads [22, 23]. The inefficient adsorption ability of these porous materials is restricted by the scarce adsorption sites. As a result, developing porous materials with high-density adsorption sites to trap LiPSs remains still a great challenge.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), composed of organic linkers and metal ion (or metal clusters), have been widely used in various applications [24–29]. By virtue of the large pore volume which can store LiPSs and the devisable adsorption sites for LiPSs, MOFs have also been applied as interlayers for LiPSs. For example, ZIF-8, which have smaller aperture sizes than LiPSs were investigated as interlayer to restrain the shuttle effect with the size exclusion effect [30]. However, the MOF-based interlayers are plagued by defects caused by the heterogeneous structure, through which the LiPSs can diffuse
across, resulting in the incomplete cut-off of the shuttle effect. In addition, although MOFs are superior in the design of host-guest interactions, how different chemical groups in the framework interact with LiPsSs and how the bottom-to-up assembling of MOFs in the interlayer affects the diffusion of LiPSs have not been fully understood. Therefore, optimal MOFs-based interlayer needs the design of both chemical functional sites and microstructure, which is still a challenge.
To target the aforementioned issues, we propose the ordered structure of functionalized MOFs as interlayer. As illustrated in Fig. 1, an ordered layer-by-layer structure constructed from MOF particles with abundant adsorption sites toward LiPSs was designed. Under this scenario, the diffusion route of LiPSs will be restrained in the ordered channels formed by MOFs between graphene sheets and LiPSs are expected to be trapped by MOF particles during the diffusion process [31]. As the host-guest interaction between MOF and LiPSs plays a key role in the inhibitory process toward shuttle effect in a MOF-constructed interlayer, the adsorption behavior of MOFs with different functional groups were firstly studied to reveal the interaction mechanism. Subsequently, the ordered laminar interlayer was constructed with \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) which has the highest adsorbability. Electrochemical measurements show that the barrier effect of the interlayer with ordered structure is superior to that with disordered structure. Compared with the undesigned battery, the Li-S battery with laminar structured interlayer improved the initial discharge capacity from 690 to \(890~\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) and lowered the decay rate from \(0.10\%\) to \(0.06\%\) at \(1.0~C,\) and the specific capacity was improved by \(96.6\%\) at \(1.0\,C\) after 500 cycles.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Preparation of the MOFs
UiO-66 was prepared according to a previously reported method [32]. Typically, 1 mmol of \(Z\mathrm{r}C1_{4}\) and 1 mmol of 1,4- benzenedicarboxylic acid were dissolved in \(5~\mathrm{\mL}\) N,Ndimethylformamide (DMF). The mixture was then transferred into a \(25~\mathrm{mL}\) Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave and heated at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) . The powders were collected by centrifugation and washed several times with DMF and methanol. Then the powders were dried overnight at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to obtain UiO-66. \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) was prepared with the same procedure by replacing 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid with 2-aminoterephthalic acid.
UiO-66-2OH was synthesized as formerly reported [33]. \(Z\mathrm{r}C1_{4}\) \(\mathrm{250~mg,~1.08~mmol)}\) and 2,5-dihydroxyterephthalic acid \((294~\mathrm{mg},\,1.5~\mathrm{mmol})\) were dissolved in a mixture of hydrochloric acid \(\left(2\,\mathrm{mL}\right)\) and DMF \(\left(30\,\mathrm{mL}\right)\) . The solution was then introduced into a \(50~\mathrm{mL}\) autoclave and heated at \(100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12~\mathrm{{h}}\) . The
resultant powders were collected via centrifugation, washed 3 times with DMF and ethanol and then dried for future use.
UiO-66-COOH was synthesized according to a previous report [34]. \(Z\mathrm{r}C1_{4}\) \(\left(1.2\ \mathrm{g},\ 5\ \mathrm{mmol}\right)\) and 1,2,4-benzenetetracarboxylic acid \(\left(2.2\ \mathrm{g},\ 10\ \mathrm{mmol}\right)\) were dissolved under stirring in \(25~\mathrm{mL}\) distilled water at room temperature. The solution was then heated at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under reflux for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) . After centrifugation and drying, UiO-66-COOH were obtained.
## 2.2 Preparation UiO-66-NH2@graphene
\(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) @graphene was prepared as follows. \(Z\mathrm{r}C1_{4}\) \(\left(240\;\mathrm{mg},\right.\) \(1\ \mathrm{mmol})\) , graphene \(\left(80~\mathrm{mg}\right)\) and polyvinyl pyrrolidone \(\left(100\;\mathrm{mg}\right)\) (PVP, \(M_{\mathrm{W}}\,{=}\,8{,}000)\) were mixed in DMF \(\left(5\mathrm{~mL}\right)\) . The mixture was stirred for \(1\,\mathrm{~h~}\) and ultrasonicated for \(10\,\mathrm{\min}\) for three cycles and then 1 mmol 2-Aminoterephthalic acid was added. The solution was then transferred into a \(25~\mathrm{mL}\) Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave and heated at \(100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) . After reaction, the synthesized UiO-66-NH2@graphene powders were washed by DMF and methanol, and dried at \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) .
## 2.3 Preparation of separators coated with different interlayers
The interlayer materials are easy to fallen off from the PP membrane without using of polymer binder. In order to construct the ordered structure without polymer binder, glass microfiber separator was chosen here. For the separator coated with UiO-66-NH2@graphene, UiO-66-NH2@graphene was firstly dispersed in \(40~\mathrm{mL}\) of ethanol and stirred for \(1\;\mathrm{{h}}\) , followed by sonication for 10 mins. The suspension was then filtered onto a glass microfiber (GF) separator by vacuum filtration. The loading density of interlayer materials was kept at \(0.30\;\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) .
The separator coated with the mixture of UiO-66-NH2 and graphene (denoted as \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\) (graphene) was fabricated using the same method. First, UiO-66-NH2 and graphene were dispersed in \(10~\mathrm{mL}\) DMF with a mass ratio of 3:1. Then the suspension was vigorously stirred for \(1\,\mathrm{~h~}\) and ultrasonicated for \(20\ \mathrm{min}\) for three times. The suspension was then filtered onto a GF separator by vacuum filtration and dried at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) overnight before use. The separator coated with graphene was fabricated using the same method, but replacing the mixture with graphene.
