## 2.5 Lithium polysulfide adsorption tests
The \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\) solution was prepared by dissolving sulfur and lithium sulfide \(\left(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\right)\) in a molar ratio of 5:1 in 1,2-dimethoxyethane (DME) and 1,3-dioxolane (DOL) mixture solution (1:1 by volume). The lithium polysulfide adsorption tests were conducted by adding respectively an equivalent amount \(\left(10\;\mathrm{mg}\right)\) of UiO-66, UiO-66-NH2,UiO-66-COOH,UiO-66-2OH,UiO-66-
\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) graphene and graphene to the solution of \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\) (0.1 M). The solutions were then kept standing for \(12\,\mathrm{{h}}\).
## 2.6  Diffusion coefficient of lithium ion
The \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) diffusion coefficient \((D_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}})\) was measured by cyclic voltammetry (CV) with different scan rates of 0.2, 0.3, 0.4 and \(0.5\;\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) and calculated based on the Randles–Sevcik equation
\[
I_{\mathrm{p}}=2.69\times10^{5}\times n^{3/2}AD_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}}^{1/2}\nu^{1/2}C_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}}
\]
where \(I_{\mathrm{p}}\) indicates the peak current, \(n\) is the number of electrons in the reaction, \(A\) is the electrode area, \(\nu\) is the scanning rate, and \(C_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}}\) is the \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) concentration in the electrolyte.
## 2.7  Material characterization
The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) were recorded by a Rigaku SmartLab X-ray diffractometer in a \(2\theta\) range of \(5^{\circ}\) to \(30^{\circ}\). The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images were obtained with Hitachi S4800 electron microscope (Japan). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements were conducted with Thermo Escalab 250 system. TG analysis of the CNT/S composite was performed in a PerkinElmer (TA Instruments) up to \(600\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) with a heating rate of \(10\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\cdot\mathrm{min}^{-1}\) in \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\). The adsorption capability of MOFs toward \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\) was characterized with the ultraviolet–visible (UV–vis) spectrometer (UV-2600).
## 2.8  Fabrication of Li-S battery and the electrochemical measurements
CR2032 Li-S coin cells were assembled using CNT/S as the cathode, functionalized GF as the separator and lithium as the anode. \(1\,\mathrm{M}\) lithium bis(trifluoromethane sulfonimide) (LiTFSI) in DOL and DME mixture (1:1 by volume) with 1 wt \(\%\) LiNO3 was used as electrolyte. The ratio of electrolyte to S for S/CNT cathode was controlled to be \(20~\upmu\mathrm{L}\cdot\mathrm{mg}^{-1}\). The galvanostatic charge-discharge tests were carried out at a voltage range of \(1.5{-}2.8\,\mathrm{V}\) using a Neware battery testing system. The CV measurements and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) were conducted with an electrochemical workstation (Autolab 302N) and the CV measurements were carried out from 1.5 to \(2.8~\mathrm{V}\) at a scan rate of \(0.1\ \mathrm{mV}\cdot\mathrm{s}^{-1}\). All the electrochemical tests were performed at room temperature.
## 3 Results and discussion
In this work, the UiO series of MOFs were selected as MOF candidates because of their physicochemical stability and chemical tunability [31]. Specifically, a series of \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs which have the same structure but different functional groups, i.e., \(-\mathrm{OH}\), \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) and –COOH, can be easily synthesized for investigating the influence of functional groups on the trap of LiPSs and performance of battery. The crystallinity of the obtained MOFs was firstly testified by the PXRD (Fig. S2 in the ESM) and the difference in ligand did not influence the crystal structure significantly. However, as shown in Fig. 2(a), MOFs with different functional groups demonstrate different adsorption capabilities toward LiPSs, and it seems that UiO\(66{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}\) performed the best as the \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\) solution with UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) as adsorbent became almost clear after \(5~\mathrm{min}\). UV–vis absorbance spectroscopy in Fig. 2(b) further verifies this observation. The peaks at 260 and \(280\;\mathrm{nm}\) are assigned to the absorption of \(S_{6}{}^{2-}\) in \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\), and therefore, the least amount of \(S_{6}{}^{2-}\) was left in the solution after the adsorption with \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), indicating that \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) has the highest adsorption capacity for LiPSs. It is also need to be noted that all the functionalized MOFs, i.e., UiO-66-2OH, UiO-66-COOH and UiO-66-NH2 demonstrate higher adsorption efficiencies compared with UiO-66. Considering that the functionalized MOFs have smaller surface areas due to the presence of the functional groups in pores, the improved adsorption properties thus can be ascribed to the interaction between LiPSs and Lewis bases in MOFs, i.e., \(-\mathrm{OH}\), –COOH and \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) groups.
and \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) demonstrate higher adsorption efficiencies compared with UiO-66. Considering that the functionalized MOFs have smaller surface areas due to the presence of the functional groups in pores, the improved adsorption properties thus can be ascribed to the interaction between LiPSs and Lewis bases in MOFs, i.e., \(-\mathrm{OH}\), –COOH and \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) groups.
XPS was applied to investigate the interaction between LiPSs and MOFs taking UiO-66-NH2 as an example. In Fig. 2(c), the peak centered at \(53.9~\mathrm{eV}\) in Li 1s spectrum of graphene/ \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\) is attributed to the Li-S bond. Whereas after polysulfide adsorption, the collected spectrum of \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\) shows an asymmetric Li 1s peak that can be deconvoluted into 2 peaks, as shown in Fig. 2(d). According to previous reports [35–37], the additional peak centered at \(56.2~\mathrm{eV}\) can be attributed to the \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) interacting with N. In the N 1s spectra of \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) (Fig. S3(a) in the ESM), the peak centered at \(399.4~\mathrm{eV}\) in N 1s spectrum is attributed to the \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) group [38]. Whereas after adsorption of polysulfide, the collected spectrum of \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\) \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\) shows an asymmetric N 1s peak that can be deconvoluted into 2 peaks. According to previous reports [36], the additional peak centered at \(398.9~\mathrm{eV}\) can be attributed to the interaction between \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) and N. For \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\), two sets of doublets located at \(163.3/164.4~\mathrm{eV}\) and \(161.3/162.8~\mathrm{eV}\) can be observed (Fig. S3(b) in the ESM), representing the atoms of bridging sulfur \((S_{\mathrm{B}}{}^{0})\) and terminal sulfur \((S_{\mathrm{T}}{}^{-1})\) [39]. After adsorption, no obvious shift can be observed for the S 2p doublets. After adsorption, blue shift can be observed for the S 2p doublets, implying the interaction between S and \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) groups. Therefore, the plentiful amino groups in \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) are expected to facilitate the adsorption of LiPSs via the strong polar interaction with \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\), explaining the highest adsorption.
As \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) demonstrates the best LiPSs adsorption capability, it was applied in the interlayer of Li-S battery. Graphene can be directly used as interlayer by virtue of its two-dimensional (2D) structure and physical barrier effect for LiPSs, and thus was investigated as a reference in this work. In order to investigate the influence of structure design of interlayers, \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\) graphene and \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) graphene were prepared. UiO-66-NH2/graphene was obtained through vacuum filtration of slurry containing dispersed MOF particles and graphene, a typical preparation route for MOF-based interlayer. Our innovation here is to construct ordered \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) graphene as interlayer in which
MOF particles were grown directly on graphene to obtain UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene and UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene was then assembled layer-by-layer on GF separator. The PXRD result in Fig. S4 in the ESM verifies the crystallinity of UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene, resembling that of UiO-66-NH\(_2\).
The enlarged cross-sectional views of both physically mixed UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene and ordered UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene are shown in Fig. S5 in the ESM, which show a thickness of 1.3 and \(1.5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\), respectively. It can be observed in Fig. 3(a) that UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene has a disordered structure as the UiO-66-NH\(_2\) nanoparticles agglomerated severely and a mass of gaps were present between the 2D graphene sheets, which is detrimental for the blocking of LiPSs. In contrast, the UiO-66-NH\(_2\) particles in ordered structure of UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene distributed uniformly on graphene sheets without agglomeration (Fig. 3(b)), and the cross-sectional view in Figs. 3(c) and 3(d) further indicates that a closely packed structure was realized with MOF particles residing between orderly arranged graphene sheets. As a result, LiPSs can only circuitously diffuse along the interlamellar space between the graphene sheets, which will greatly prolong the diffusion route of LiPSs. Moreover, according to our design scheme in Fig. 1, during the diffusion process, LiPSs are expected to be trapped by the highly adsorptive UiO-66-NH\(_2\).
The adsorption experiments in Fig. S6 in the ESM verified that the superior adsorption capability of UiO-66-NH\(_2\) for
\(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\) was retained after compositing with graphene as the \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\) solution with UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene quickly became clear after \(5~\mathrm{min}\), whereas the color of \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\) solution with graphene did not change appreciably. Further permeation experiments were conducted with interlayers coated on GF separator, i.e., UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene/GF separator, UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene/GF separator and graphene/GF separator using an H-shaped device. As shown in Fig. 4(a), graphene/GF demonstrates the worst barrier effect as the lucid electrolyte solvent (in the right chamber of the device) changed from colorless to yellow-brown after \(12~\mathrm{h}\), indicating rapid diffusion of LiPSs through the separator. In comparison, slight color change was observed in the right chamber with UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene as interlayer, indicating that although UiO-66-NH\(_2\) has a high adsorption ability for LiPSs, the LiPSs can still slowly penetrate the separator due to the gaps between the agglomerated MOF particles and disordered graphene sheets in the interlayer. Interestingly, no color change was observed in the case of UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene, revealing that the ordered structure did greatly improve the barrier effect by prolonging the diffusion route as well as restraining the gaps in the interlayer. Thus, it can be concluded that both the high adsorption capacity of the material and a proper design of the ordered microstructure are necessary for the efficient barrier effect.
To understand the impact of barrier effect on the performance of lithium-sulfur battery, the electrochemical impedance measurements of batteries with different interlayers were conducted. The results are summarized in Fig. 4(b), and the corresponding equivalent circuit for fitting the Nyquist plots is drawn in Fig. 4(c), referring to previous report [40]. The intersection of the Nyquist plot and the \(\chi\)-axis indicates the Ohmic resistance \((R_{\mathrm{e}})\) between the electrode and the electrolyte. The \(R_{\mathrm{e}}\) would reflect the Ohmic resistance of the whole battery between the electrode and the electrolyte. In Li-S battery, the concentration of the LiPSs in the electrolyte contributes only a part of \(R_{\mathrm{e}}\). The polysulfide may penetrate to the anodic side and \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) will form due to the side reaction between Li anode and deposit on the surface of Li metal, which will also increase the value of \(R_{\mathrm{e}}\). The two semicircles of the Nyquist plot reflect the charge-transfer resistance at the cathode \((R_{\mathrm{ct}})\) and interfacial layer resistance \((R_{\mathrm{il}})\). Specifically, \(R_{\mathrm{il}}\) will increase drastically without suppressing the shuttle effect. The polysulfides will be adsorbed on the separator and converted to solid lithium sulfide during the charge-discharge process, leading to an interface contact resistance between solid lithium sulfide and
cathode. After charging to \(2.8\,\mathrm{~V~}\), the battery with UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) graphene as interlayer possess the lowest \(R_{\mathrm{e}}\) value \((3.13\;\Omega)\), demonstrating that the polysulfide can be obstructed on the side of cathode rather than diffuse to the anodic side. However, for the batteries with UiO-66-NH\(_2\)/graphene and graphene as interlayers, they show increased \(R_{\mathrm{e}}\) value, revealing that these interlayers allow the polysulfide penetrate to the anodic side and \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) will form due to the side reaction between Li anode and deposit on the surface of Li metal. The semicircle for the UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene interlayer has the smallest diameter, indicating that the corresponding battery has the lowest resistance. By contrast, the resistance existing in the battery with UiO-66-NH\(_2\)/graphene as interlayer is much higher, confirming that the ordered MOFs' interlayer is superior than disordered structure for restricting LiPSs' diffusion.
The rate performances of Li-S batteries fabricated with different separators were evaluated, which are shown in Fig. 5(a). The initial discharge capacity of \(1598\,\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) for UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene interlayer was delivered at \(0.2\,\mathrm{C}\) ( \(1.0\,\mathrm{C}=\) \(1,675\,\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\)). With increasing the current density to \(0.5\,\mathrm{C}\), \(1.0\,\mathrm{C}\), \(2.0\,\mathrm{C}\) and \(3.0\,\mathrm{C}\), discharge capacities of 1,100, 900, 725, \(705\,\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) were obtained, respectively. In comparison, the battery with UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene as interlayer displayed discharge capacities 1,400, 900, 760, 700 and \(500\,\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) at \(0.2\,\mathrm{C}\), \(0.5\,\mathrm{C}\), \(1.0\,\mathrm{C}\), \(2.0\,\mathrm{C}\) and \(3.0\,\mathrm{C}\), respectively, lower than that of UiO-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene. The batteries with graphene/GF and pristine GF separator displayed even lower discharge capacities. Compared with the interlayer with disorder structure, the interlayer with ordered structure can improve the discharge capacity by \(41\%\) at \(3\,\mathrm{C}\). In addition, the batteries with ordered interlayers constructed with different MOFs were also investigated (Fig. S7 in the ESM). The performance of the battery with UiO-66-NH\(_2\) is superior than the that of the batteries with other MOFs, which matches well with the best adsorption capacity of UiO-66-NH\(_2\). Therefore, the improved barrier effect of the interlayer restrained the shuttle process of LiPSs and leads to significant improvement of the rate performances.
The cyclic voltammetry curves of all cells shown in Fig. 5(b) exhibit two obvious reduction peaks at \(\sim\,2.3\) and \(\sim\,2.0\,\mathrm{~V}\), corresponding to the transformation from \(S_{8}\) to long-chain polysulfides and the further reduction of polysulfides to \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\). It can be seen that UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene had more positive potentials during the reduction process and more
negative potentials during the oxidation process compared with UiO-66-NH\(_2\)/graphene and graphene interlayer, indicating the smallest polarization of the cell with UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene. The results are compatible with the voltage hysteresis \((\Delta E)\) in the charge-discharge profiles (Fig. 5(c)). The \(\Delta E\) of the cell with UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene is \(0.25\,\mathrm{eV}\), smaller than that of 0.33 and \(0.31\,\mathrm{eV}\) with UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene and graphene interlayer, respectively. These results demonstrate better inhibitory effect of ordered UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene interlayer for shuttle effect during the discharge of Li-S batteries.
The self-discharge test was conducted to further investigate how the composite separators influence the performance of Li-S batteries in Fig. S8 in the ESM. The self-discharge, which is resulted from the shuttle of soluble LiPSs, leads to irreversible decay of specific capacity and reduction of the open-circuit voltage (OCV). The Li-S batteries with graphene, UiO-66-NH\(_2\)/graphene and UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene as interlayer were tested by discharging to \(2.15\,\mathrm{V}\) and resting for \(10\,\mathrm{h}\) in the \(2^{\mathrm{nd}}-4^{\mathrm{th}}\) cycles. After the \(4^{\mathrm{th}}\) charge, the batteries were rested for \(72\,\mathrm{h}\) and the OCV reduction was studied. Compared with the other batteries, the discharge specific capacity of the battery with UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene as interlayer displays the lowest decrease \((8.20\%)\) and displays a higher initial voltage \((2.33\,\mathrm{V})\) after the \(4^{\mathrm{th}}\) cycles (Fig. S8(c) in the ESM). These results imply that the ordered structure of layered UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene has excellent anti-self-discharge behavior by effectively suppressing the shuttle effect of LiPSs.
To further investigate the efficiency of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) diffusion in UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene and UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene. The Li-ion diffusion coefficient \((D_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}})\) was measured by CV at different scan rates of 0.2, 0.3, 0.4 and \(0.5\,\mathrm{mV{\cdot}s^{-1}}\) and calculated based on the Randles–Sevcik equation [41]. As shown in Fig. S9 in the ESM, the peak currents increase with the increasement of scan rate. All the peak currents of cathodic and anodic have a linear relation with the square root of scan rates \((\upsilon^{1/2})\). The calculated diffusion coefficients are listed in Table S1 in the ESM, demonstrating that the battery with UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene as interlayer shows higher \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) diffusion coefficients. These results show that the interlayer of UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene has the best block effect without hampering the diffusion of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\).
The effectiveness of restraining shuttle effect of polysulfide with UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene and UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\(@\)graphene was also investigated using long cycling tests. As shown in Fig. 5(d),
the initial capacity of the Li-S battery with GF separator was \(709\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) and dropped to \(317\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) after 500 cycles at \(1.0\,\mathrm{C}\), which can be attributed to the severe polysulfide shuttle, leading to the low utilization of active sulfur and the corrosion of lithium cathode. In comparison, the initial capacity of Li-S battery with \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}@}\) graphene as interlayer was \(890\,\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) and retained \(800\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) after 100 cycles at \(1.0\,\mathrm{C}\), accounting for \(89.8\%\) of the initial value. After 500 cycles, the capacity slowly declined to \(590\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\), corresponding to a decay rate of \(0.067\%\) per cycle. The Li-S battery used \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}@}\) graphene as interlayer showed an initial capacity of \(790\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) and became \(502\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) after 500 cycles at \(1.0\,\mathrm{C}\), rendering an attenuation rate of \(0.10\%\) per cycle. Compared to \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}@}\) graphene, the inferior battery performance may be ascribed to the agglomeration of MOF particles in interlayer and the gaps between graphene sheets, which again confirms the efficiency of the ordered structure in \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}@}\) graphene interlayer. Nevertheless, both the \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}@}\) graphene interlayer and the \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}@}\) graphene interlayers have improved cycling performance of Li-S batteries compared with the graphene interlayer and the pure GF separator, demonstrating the contribution of the adsorption property of \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}}\) in obstructing the shuttle effect of polysulfide. It should be noted that the battery performances obtained with the ordered structure of interlayer are highly competitive among the reported MOF-based interlayers as shown in Table S2 and Fig. S10 in the ESM. On the other hand, the SEM image of the ordered interlayer after cycling shows that the interlayer kept stable on the separator (Fig. S11(a) in the ESM) and its thickness is almost the same compared with the thickness before cycling (\(1.26\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) vs. \(1.30\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\), Fig. S11(b) in the ESM). Moreover, the interlayer kept good contact with the substrate separator after being immersed in the electrolyte for three days (Fig. S11(c) in the ESM). All these tests prove the stability of the ordered interlayer during the cycling.
To evaluate the potential of \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}@}\) graphene interlayer for practical Li-S batteries, the electrochemical properties of Li-S batteries with higher sulfur loading (\(3.5\ \mathrm{mg{\cdot}cm^{-2}}\)) was studied. The corresponding \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}@}\) graphene/GF separator was used to assemble Li-S battery. After 100 cycles at \(0.1\ \mathrm{C}\), the discharge capacities of Li-S batteries with \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}@}\) graphene/GF separator and pristine GF separator were 980 and \(800\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\), respectively. Meanwhile, \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}@}\) graphene/GF separator exhibited a low capacity attenuation rate of \(0.067\%\) per cycle. The battery performance under high S load thus suggests the potential of \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}@}\) graphene/GF separator in Li-S batteries for practical use.
## 4 Conclusions
In this work, a strategy which combines the design of both chemical interaction and microstructure of interlayer was put forward to improve the performance of Li-S batteries. The influence of different functionalized MOFs for the capture of LiPSs was systematically studied and the results show that \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}}\) possesses the highest adsorption capacity for LiPSs. Furthermore, an interlayer with ordered structure was designed and successfully fabricated with \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}}\) to suppress the shuttle effects of LiPSs in Li-S battery. The ordered structure of \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}}\)-based interlayer can not only prolong the diffusion routes but also trap LiPSs in pores of MOFs by the chemical interaction between MOF and LiPSs. The ordered structure of interlayer can obviously improve the capacity and lifetime of the Li-S battery. Compared with the
undersigned battery, the Li-S battery with laminar structured interlayer improved the initial discharge capacity by \(28.9\%\) and lowered the decay rate by \(40\%\) at \(1.0\,\mathrm{C}\), and the specific capacity was improved by \(96.6\%\) at \(1.0\,\mathrm{C}\) after 500 cycles. This work thus facilitates the understanding of the interaction mechanism between MOFs and LiPSs. Most importantly, the strategy about ordered structure of MOFs-based interlayer proposed in this contribution paves a new avenue for the design of high-performance interlayer, which would be widely applicable for other polar materials which can trap LiPSs.
